organize our work according to the 5 strategic priority areas identified in the Global strategy and action plan on ageing and health 2016-2020.\nL81: \nL82: 【65†About us】\nL83: # Integrated Continuum of Long-term care\nL84: ### Providing access to long-term care for older people\nL85: \nL86: Older people continue to have aspirations to well-being and respect regardless of declines in physical and mental capacity. Long-term-care systems enable older people, who experience significant declines in capacity, to receive the care and support that allow them to live a life consistent with their basic rights, fundamental freedoms and human dignity.\nL87: These services can also help reduce the inappropriate use of acute health-care services, help families avoid catastrophic care expenditures and free women – usually the main caregivers – to have broader social roles. While global data on the need and unmet need for long-term care do not exist, national-level data reveal large gaps in the provision of and access to such services in many low- and middle-income countries.\nL88: ### What is long-term care?\nL89: \nL90: Long-term care services include traditional health service such as management of chronic geriatric conditions, rehabilitation, palliation, promotion and preventative services.\nL91: \nL92: However, long-term care services should also include assistive care services such as caregiving and social support for older people. All these services must be integrated and provided in a continuum with the underlying core principles of person-centred care.\nL93: 【3†13 March 2015 WHO: Dementia – it affects us all】\nL94: ## What is WHO doing for long-term care?\nL95: \nL96: * * *\nL97: ###\nL98: \nL99: WHO Integrated Continuum of Long-term care will uphold these principles of integration, non-fragmentation and person-centredness to support countries develop quality long-term care programmes.\nL100: \nL101: WHO has identified three approaches that will be crucial. These are:\nL102:   * establishing the foundations necessary for provision of long-term care as part of universal health coverage;\nL103:   * building and maintaining a sustainable and appropriately trained workforce and supporting unpaid caregivers; and\nL104:   * ensuring the quality of long-term care.\nL105: \nL106:  To support these approaches WHO will:\nL107:   * provide technical support for national situation analyses of long-term care and for development, implementation and monitoring of relevant legislation, policies, plans, financing and services; and\nL108:   * design tools and guidance for a minimum package of long-term care as part of universal health coverage, including:\nL109:     * provide online resources for informal caregivers;\nL110:     * improve the working conditions of care workers; and\nL111:     * assess the health impact of social protection programmes\nL112: 【66†Image: Healthy ageing†cdn.who.int】\nL113: \nL114: A. Cohn\nL115: \nL116: © Credits\nL117: \nL118: WHO long-term care series\nL119: \nL120: The WHO long-term care series aims to catalyse change and encourage the development of sustainable and equitable long-term care systems worldwide. The series will do this by sharing regional experiences of long-term care, including gaps, challenges, models of care and support worth considering; and providing guidance on key issues, such as financing, human resourcing, monitoring and research.\nL121: 【67†Towards long-term care systems in sub-Saharan Africa】\nL122: \nL123: \nL124: \nL125: 【68†Image: Health ageing†cdn.who.int】\nL126: \nL127: R. Waddington\nL128: \nL129: © Credits\nL130: \nL131: Regional discussions on long-term care\nL132: At the World Health Assembly in May 2016, 194 countries agreed that every country should have a long-term-care system. Yet few countries have systems in place that can adequately meet the care and support needs of their populations. Long-term-care systems that are sustainable and equitable can take different forms depending on the cultural and economic context.\nL133: WHO is holding regional policy dialogues to build understanding on what is needed and commitment to take action that will meet the needs of frail and care-dependent older people.\nL134: \nL135: \nL136: 【69†Image: Age friendly cities and communities†cdn.who.int】\nL137: \nL138: Global Network on Long-term Care\nL139: The WHO Global Network on Long-term care (GNLTC) is a multidisciplinary, multi-institutional network of experts that provides strategic and technical advice to WHO in developing norms and guidelines necessary for the implementation of the WHO Global Strategy and Action Plan on Ageing and Health in the area of long-term care. GNLTC will advise WHO on ways to create equitable and sustainable long-term care models for older people, with a focus on optimizing functional ability and achieving Healthy Ageing.\nL218: ## Additional publications linked to COVID-19\nL219: \nL220: More resources for care for older persons in the context of COVID-19:   【92†Access here】\nL235: 【96†Image: Rapid hospital readiness checklist: Interim Guidance†iris.who.int】\nL236: \nL237: 26 November 2020\nL238: \nL239: 【97†Rapid hospital readiness checklist: Interim Guidance】\nL240: \nL241: Access the tool: Rapid hospital readiness checklist: tool Use and content Countries can use this checklist of hospital governance, structures, plans and...\nL242: \nL243: 【98†Download†iris.who.int】\nL244: \nL245: 【97†Read More】\nL246: \nL247: 【99†Image: Infection prevention and control health-care facility response for COVID-19†iris.who.int】\nL248: \nL249: 20 October 2020\nL250: 【100†Infection prevention and control health-care facility response for COVID-19】\nL251: \nL252: Use and contentThis self-assessment tool is designed for acute health-care facilities (i.e. tertiary and secondary) but can be modified for the use in...\nL253: \nL254: 【101†Download†iris.who.int】\nL255: \nL256: 【100†Read More】\nL257: \nL258: Journal articles\nL259: 【102†Artificial intelligence for older people receiving long-term care: a systematic review of acceptability and effectiveness studies Artificial intelligence for older people receiving long-term care: a systematic review of acceptability and effectiveness studies Lancet Healthy Longev 2022; 3: e286–97†www.thelancet.com】 【103†A recommended package of Long-Term Care Services to promote healthy ageing based on a WHO Global Expert Consensus Study A recommended package of Long-Term Care Services to promote healthy ageing based on a WHO Global Expert Consensus Study JAMDA 23 (2022) 297 - 303†www.jamda.com】 【104†Implementing care for healthy ageing Implementing care for healthy ageing BMJ Global Health 2022;7:e007778†gh.bmj.com】\nL260: Focus on dementia\nL261: \nL262: 【105†26 October 2026 Dementia】\nL263: \nL264: 【106†Infographic Ensuring that the person with dementia continues to receive care†cdn.who.int】\nL265: \nL266: 【107†Infographic Dementia: a public health priority†cdn.who.int】\nL267: \nL268: 【108†All infographics on dementia】\nL269: \nL270: iSupport for Dementia\nL271: Caregiving for people with dementia can take a heavy physical and emotional toll. To help caregivers, WHO has launched iSupport, a support programme. iSupport helps caregivers understand the impact of dementia, deal with challenging behaviour, provide good care, and take care of themselves.\nL272: \nL273: 【109†Training and support manual for carers of people with dementia】\nL274: \nL275: \nL276: \nL277: Related activities\nL278: \nL279: 【110†Providing access to long-term care for older people】\nL280: \nL281: Related health topic\nL282: \nL283: 【111†Populations and demographics Ageing】\nL284: 【112†Newsletters】\nL285: \nL286: 【113†Report misconduct】\nL287: \nL288: 【114†Privacy policy】\nL289: \nL290: © 2026\nL291: \nL292: 【115†WHO】\nL293: \nL294: [Input]\n"
